WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:06.000
Did you ever wonder if certain foods
have a direct effect on your health?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Well, I’m here to
solve that mystery

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.440
since you are
the foods you eat.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.480
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.600
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.120
Today, we look at why it’s so hard
to outrun a bad diet.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.480
When trying to lose weight,
which is more important:

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.200
diet or exercise?

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.400
A national survey found that
a "vast majority" of Americans

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.520
trying to control their weight

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.400
believed that food
and beverage consumption

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.720
and physical activity
were equally important.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.720
Seven out of ten
went with equally important,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.320
about two out of ten thought
exercise was more important,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.120
and only about one
out of ten chose diet.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.880
The vast majority of Americans
are wrong.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.600
It's easy to understand
how people might think

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.840
diet and exercise
play equal roles.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.480
After all,
our weight is determined

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.880
by the balance of calories in
and calories out.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.200
What people may not understand

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.320
about this
energy balance equation

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.120
is we have much more power
over the "calories in" side.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.920
In fact, on a day-to-day basis
we have full control,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.480
we could choose
to eat zero calories

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.160
or 10,000 calories.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.120
But most of the "calories out"

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.120
tend to be outside
of our control.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.960
Unlike wild animals
who tend to burn

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.840
most of their calories
on activity,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.640
about 60 percent
of our daily calories

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.240
are used up
just to keep us alive,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.960
what's called our resting,
or basal, metabolic rate,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.200
thanks in part
to our energy-intensive brains.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.960
Even if you stayed in bed
all day,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.560
you'd still burn
more than 1,000 calories

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.400
just to fuel the basics
like thinking, breathing,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.400
keeping your heart pumping.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.040
In contrast,
even most "active" people

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.720
accrue less than two hours
of exercise a week,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.520
which may average out
to be less than 100 calories

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.440
burned off each day.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.880
That's only about 5 percent

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.560
of the total
daily energy expenditure,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.440
the "calories out" side
of the equation.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.920
So, the 2,000 calories we may
take in every day from our diet

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.160
can exert 20 times
more influence than exercise

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.120
over our weight destiny.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.640
Most people believe that
exercise is "very effective"

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.240
as a way to lose weight,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.240
but this has been
referred to as a myth

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.720
in the scientific literature.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.760
In fact, it's been labeled

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.160
one of the most common
misconceptions

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.240
in the field of obesity.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.200
Yet virtually all
formal weight-loss guidelines

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.800
include some sort of physical
activity recommendation.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.200
Can you outrun a bad diet?

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.560
Let's see what the science says.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.360
Population studies
have certainly found

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.960
strong correlations between
physical inactivity and obesity.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.120
But does a sedentary lifestyle
lead to obesity,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.520
or does obesity lead
to a sedentary lifestyle?

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.920
It probably works a little bit
in both directions.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.040
To prove cause and effect,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.040
and also quantify
the relationship,

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.840
you really have to put it
to the test.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.440
Dozens of randomized
controlled trials

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.000
involving thousands
of participants

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.720
have been published
on the effects of exercise

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.080
on weight loss.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.920
Physical activity was not found
to be an effective strategy.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.120
For example,
if you look at the studies

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.560
that tried using exercise alone
to induce weight loss,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.440
over an average
of about five months,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.960
people only lost
about three pounds.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.200
When you put
all the studies together,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.680
it looks like it took
around six weeks of exercising

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.160
to get people
to lose a single pound.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.600
That was exercise alone, though.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.520
What about as an adjunct
to diet?

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.560
If you randomize people

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.400
into a diet
and exercise intervention

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.280
versus just the diet alone,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.920
the added exercise group
does do better,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.560
but the difference
in weight loss

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.000
only averaged about two pounds.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.440
The studies lasted
between 3 and 12 months,

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.400
and all of that
extra prescribed exercise

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.440
only seemed to translate
into a few pounds.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.800
The two-pound difference
was statistically significant,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.080
which means we're pretty sure
it was a real effect,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.520
but losing two pounds
over a year's time

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.480
can hardly be considered
clinically significant.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.280
As a general rule,
researchers like to see

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.760
at least a five-
or six-pound drop.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.440
In a meta-analysis of 18
randomized controlled trials

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.560
lasting a minimum
of six months,

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.320
the diet-plus-exercise group

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.160
failed to significantly beat out
the diet-only group at all.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.640
There appeared to be
no long-term benefit

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.560
to encouraging people
to add exercise

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.360
to their weight-loss regimen.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.240
What is going on?

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.800
Maybe exercise is just better

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.040
at preventing people
from regaining weight.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:18.720
No.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.680
The vast majority
of randomized controlled trials

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.320
examining
weight-loss maintenance

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.240
also failed to show
an exercise benefit.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.320
Part of the problem
is compliance.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.280
It's one thing
to tell people

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.280
to adhere
to an exercise regimen;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.960
it's another thing for them
to actually do it.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.480
When the same randomized
controlled trials

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.440
were re-analyzed
to exclude people

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.120
who flouted the instructions,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.840
and analysis was limited
to just those

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.880
who actually put in
the time and sweat,

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.960
a clear advantage
to exercise emerges.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.280
Exercise only works
if you actually do it,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.520
though one reason people
may become rapidly disillusioned

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:53.880
with their new gym membership

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.720
is their gross overestimation

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.200
of the capacity of exercise
to burn off extra calories.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.240
A slice of pizza
has about 300 calories.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.360
That converts into an hour
of brisk walking per slice,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.040
an hour a slice!

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.080
How many kids
are jogging two hours a day

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.640
to burn off their happy meals?

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.920
Who's got time to climb
50 flights of stairs

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.080
to take care of the calories
in just one Oreo cookie?

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.040
That's one of the reasons

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.480
that what we put in our mouths
is most important.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.480
Public health researchers
have been experimenting

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.831
with including labeling
on junk food.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.560
Still want that Toblerone
if it means you have to walk

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.760
two hours?

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.000
Or cookies
you'd have to skip rope

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.880
for 81 minutes for?

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.360
I think I'd run an hour
just to avoid having to eat

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.920
shrimp-flavored potato chips.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.320
Labeling fast food menus
with little pictograms

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.440
of exercising stick figures
was found to help nudge people

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.480
towards lower calorie options.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.280
Seeing that the decision
to supersize your fries

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.640
would mean walking
an extra three miles that day

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.200
or that choosing
the chicken salad

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.920
over the garden salad
could mean having to run

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.280
nearly three miles,

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.480
people are more likely
to make the healthier choice.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.400
In our next story,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:12.000
we look how calories burned
don’t necessarily equal calories lost.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.160
Most overweight individuals

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.880
evidently tend
to choose exercise

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.160
as their first approach
to weight loss.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.880
When unrealistic hopes
clash with reality,

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.200
the disappointment
may lead to an abandonment

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.040
of weight-loss efforts
altogether

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.400
as an exercise in futility,
no pun intended.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:32.600
Our false expectations may also
give us license to overeat.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.600
Our pie-in-the-sky notions
about the power of exercise

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.960
may just be used to justify
an extra slice of pie

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.720
right here on earth.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.720
Some researchers warn
that labeling menus

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.600
with calorie equivalents
of exercise

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.560
could be counterproductive,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.720
backfiring if people
rationalize their indulgences

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:51.320
after a workout.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:54.720
This concern has actually been
put to the test.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.200
Exercise psychologists
took a group men and women,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.480
put them on a stationary bike,

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.160
and had them cycle
until they burned

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.880
either 50 calories
or more than 250 calories.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.520
Unbeknownst to them,
the researchers

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.440
manipulated the machines
to give false readouts,

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.440
such that in actuality

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.360
both groups burned
the same number of calories.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:16.800
They just thought
they burned more or less.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.680
Then, they were offered a meal
10 minutes later,

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:20.000
ostensibly to measure

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.520
the "effects of exercise
on taste perception,"

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.120
but the real purpose
was to covertly measure

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.720
how much people ate.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.160
Those who falsely believed

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:29.520
they had burned off
more calories

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.520
did seem to demonstrate
a greater "license to eat,"

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.160
ending up eating
significantly more calories,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.680
mostly in the form
of chocolate chip cookies.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.520
After a workout,
people may be tempted

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.720
to treat themselves
for their sweaty sacrifice.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.280
To prevent
this kneejerk reaction

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.640
from undermining our efforts,

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.760
we should strive to make
exercise less of a chore.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.120
In a paper entitled
"Is it fun or exercise?

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.880
The framing of physical activity
biases subsequent snacking,"

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.960
a study is described in which
individuals were randomized

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.720
to the same amount
of physical activity

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.800
but just described differently.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.280
Half were told they were going
on a "scenic walk,"

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:07.240
and the other half were told

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.720
they were going on
an "exercise walk."

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.640
Afterwards,
researchers covertly measured

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.560
how much dessert everyone took
at a subsequent meal.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.800
Those in the
movement-as-exercise group

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:17.960
reportedly served themselves

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.560
about 35 percent more
chocolate pudding

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.960
than the movement-as-fun group.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.840
This is all the more reason
to choose activities

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:25.880
that are enjoyable,

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.440
such as walking with friends
or while listening to music,

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.080
or watching a video
on the treadmill.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.440
Reframing exercise as play
rather than work

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.200
may not only make
for a more sustainable regimen

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.720
but may make us less likely

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.160
to consciously
or unconsciously feel the need

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.840
to later reward ourselves
at the buffet line.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.120
Even just thinking
about exercise

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.360
may compel people
to eat more food.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.240
Those randomized to simply read
about physical activity

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.280
went on to serve themselves
nearly 60 percent more M&amp;Ms

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.640
than those
in the control group,

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.280
adding up to hundreds
of extra calories.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.440
The researchers concluded:
"simply imagining exercise

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.880
leads participants to serve
themselves more food."

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.440
Expending energy
through exercise

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.000
may not just psychologically
predispose us to eat more

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.400
but may physiologically
make us hungrier.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.440
We evolved in the context
of scarcity,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.280
so our body
places great value

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:21.760
on rapidly replenishing
lost fat stores.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.720
This helps explain why
the average weight loss

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:24.680
with exercise training

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.040
is only 30 percent
of that predicted

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:29.520
based on the number
of extra calories burned.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.440
Calories in
versus calories out

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:32.840
can be complicated
by the fact

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.080
that changes on one side
of the equation can affect the other side.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.160
In other words,
we can work up an appetite.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:40.360
Carefully controlled studies

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.680
show that caloric intake
tends to rise over time

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.520
to match any increase
in caloric expenditure,

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:48.400
making significant weight loss
through exercise alone

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.280
remarkably difficult.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.520
This doesn't happen
over a day or two.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:53.600
After a workout,

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.960
there may not be
an immediate increase in hunger,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.400
but averaged over the week
or weeks,

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:01.880
our appetite does tend
to increase to balance out

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.840
most of the extra calories
we've been burning.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.080
This calorie compensation
isn't perfect, though,

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.840
so we can end up
with a net loss in body fat,

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.360
particularly at
higher exercise levels.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.360
So, the secret to weight loss
through exercise

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:16.880
may be sheer volume,

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.480
at least 300 minutes a week

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:21.160
to achieve
appreciable fat loss.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.360
This regulation of our appetite
through activity

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:25.080
works in both directions.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.400
Just as there exists
a higher level of exercise

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.400
where we can start to outpace
our appetite and lose weight,

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.480
there's a lower level
of exercise

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.160
where our body
loses the ability

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.600
to sufficiently downgrade
our appetite,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.680
and we gain weight.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.680
This sedentary zone
where our appetite

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.520
becomes uncoupled
from our activity level

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:46.760
appears to start
at around 7,100 steps a day.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:49.480
So, let's say you start out
as a really active person,

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.200
chowing down on
nearly 2,900 calories a day,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.440
and, for whatever reason,
have to cut back on exercise.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.680
You'd think you'd gain
a lot of weight,

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:00.080
but you're surprised
that you don't.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:04.026
Basically, no increased odds
of gaining significant body fat.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:08.320
With your drop in exercise came
an inadvertent drop in appetite.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.840
But there's a limit to how far
our appetite can drop.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.480
Once you cross that threshold,

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.560
once you dip below logging
at least 7,100 steps or so

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.160
a day on your pedometer,

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.640
your appetite doesn't slow
much further to match,

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.080
and the pounds can start
to pile on.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.600
Your body tries
to keep your weight steady

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:26.840
by adjusting your appetite,

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.000
but we just weren't designed

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:31.360
to handle such extreme
low levels of movement

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:36.497
that sadly characterizes
most of the U.S. population.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.840
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.320
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.960
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:49.200
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:53.080
To see any graphs, charts, graphics,
images, or studies mentioned here,

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.640
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.020
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:03.000
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.560
My latest book, “How Not to Age”
is out now,

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.280
which premiered at #2 on the
New York Times Best Sellers list.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:11.520
Check it out at your
local public library.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:13.920
Of course, all the proceeds
I receive of the sales

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:17.680
of all my books goes
directly to charity.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:22.320
NutritionFacts.org is itself a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.160
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:28.240
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.480
Everything on the
website is free.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.480
There’s no ads,

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:33.180
no corporate sponsorships,
no kickbacks.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.400
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.480
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.840
as a tribute to
my grandmother,

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.181
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

